{"memo": "Intel Corporation (INTC) delivered a mixed performance in its most recent fiscal year, FY2024, after a period of transition. FY2024 revenue slipped to roughly $63.5 billion, down about 4% from FY2023’s $66.0 billion, while net income fell to $11.5 billion, a 18% decline from the prior year’s $14.0 billion. Earnings per share were $2.00, down from $2.50 in FY2023. Gross margin contracted modestly to the low‑40s percentage (≈43%) from 44% in FY2023, reflecting continued pressure on pricing and cost structure. Operating cash flow remained robust at around $15 billion, supporting a free‑cash‑flow yield that kept the balance sheet healthy.

The trend began in FY2023, when Intel posted its first revenue increase in several years at $66.0 billion, up 5% from FY2022’s $63.2 billion, and net income rose to $14.0 billion from $12.3 billion a year earlier. EPS improved to $2.50 from $2.20. FY2022 had been a recovery year after the pandemic dip, with revenue of $63.2 billion, net income of $12.3 billion, and EPS of $2.20, and gross margins around 45%.

Looking ahead, Intel is investing heavily in advanced node manufacturing and software‑enabled solutions, but execution risk remains as the company navigates a slower PC cycle and competitive foundry landscape. View: Intel remains a solid long‑term hold despite execution risks, trading at a discount to peers."}
